A||N||2||FieroRacer||85 GT Cradle Removal||||1||
Z||000000||FieroRacer||11-14-2001||12:10 AM||||There's a good chance I will have access to a garage equiped with almost any tool needed to do most car work.  What's the best procedure for engine cradle, then engine removal?  I will have at least one helper.  None of us has ever done a mid-engine vehicle before, just want a few hints before we try anything.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroMGDriver||11-14-2001||12:35 AM||||I just got done pulling the motor and cradle from my 86 GT.  Its not that bad, and I did it in my driveway with nothing more than basic tools plus an engine hoist.  If you have access to a lift, it would make life a little easier for you.  Once you have all the hoses, cables, etc disconnected from then engine, it is just a matter of either raising the car off, or lowing the cradle out.  You should check out <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  to look at some pictures of the removal.  This is the method I used and it works well.  Make sure you have a Haynes manual to reference so you don't forget anything.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||shark93726||11-14-2001||12:58 AM||||get about 12 feet of 2x6, cut it into 4 pieces, so that the cross pieces sit under the cradle crosspieces, with the back one long enough to support the A-arms.  Put good heavy casters under each corner, with at least two swivel ones. <P>jack the whole car up and set jackstands under the front crossmember and under the front edges of the cradle.  Disconnect the battery cables and lay them on the motor.  If you have air conditioning, and it works well, you can remove the pump without disconnecting the lines, which should keep the charge if you are careful, otherwise you can unscrew the hose connections and tie the hoses up by the trunk lid hinges.<P>disconnect the wiring harness from the computer that goes to the motor and pull it back throught the hole and lay it on the motor.  Do likewise with all the other wiring from the motor.  Some individual ones may best be removed from motor and tied to body instead.  Do likewise with both heater hoses and both radiator hoses.<P>Disconnect the fuel lines from the motor, the throttle linkages and shift linkages from the transaxle.  Mark where each piece goes as you do all this. Remove the clutch hydraulic slave cylinder.  Remove the engine strut brace.  remove wheels, then remove brake calipers and tie them up in the wheel wells.<P>I probably forgot something, but you get the general idea.  Isolate every connection from the motor and transaxle to the body.<P>then drop the car down so the cradle sits on your cradle dolly.  (probably only the rear will sit on it at first) Remove the two back cradle4 bolts first.  Then jack up the front of the cradle dolly so it just supports the cradly at the front.  Now remove the other two front cradle bolts and slowly lower the cradle down on the dolly.<P>Now you use either an engine hoist or a chain hoist to lift the body up off the entire engine/transaxle/cradle/dolly.  You can hook this onto the trunk latch bar as long as you don't jerk the thing too much when raising it.  Watch very carefully for clearance and so that you didn't forget a disconnect anywhere.  once the body is high enough you should be able to roll the dolly out sideways through one wheel well area.<P>That's it.  The reverse of this procedure should put it back together.<P>Have fun.<P>Gerald Storvik   ||||reg||1
